"Saving Foundation Flow-Matching Priors for Inverse Problems (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16520v1)\nciteturn2513view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16520v1\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 507\nL149: But finite-sample training with polynomially many samples in practice causes a significant gap: due to CoM of Gaussian vectors (cite87†Theorem 3.1 ), virtually all training samples drawn from $\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$ come from an ultra-thin shell $\\mathcal{S}$ around $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})$,^{1}^{1} 1 To be precise, for $m$ iid drawn Gaussian vectors $\\boldsymbol{z}_{1},\\dots,\\boldsymbol{z}_{m}$, $\\mathbb{P}[\\exists i\\in\\left\\{1,\\dots,m\\right\\}\\;\\text{with}\\;|\\norm{\\mb z_i}_{2}-\\sqrt{d}|\\geq t]\\leq 2me^{-ct^{2}}=2e^{-ct^{2}+\\log m}$ by a simple union bound.\nL150: Taking $t=\\varepsilon\\sqrt{d}$, we obtain that $\\mathbb{P}[\\exists i\\in\\left\\{1,\\dots,m\\right\\}\\;\\text{with}\\;|\\norm{\\mb z_i}_{2}-\\sqrt{d}|\\geq\\varepsilon\\sqrt{d}]\\leq 2e^{-c\\varepsilon^{2}d+\\log m}\\leq 2e^{-c\\varepsilon^{2}d/2}$ provided that $m\\leq e^{c\\varepsilon^{2}d/2}$.\nL151: so the generation function $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ is effectively trained on inputs from the domain $\\mathcal{S}$, not the entire ambient space—which implies that the behavior of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ on $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$, the complement of $\\mathcal{S}$, is largely undetermined.\nL152: Now, samples from $\\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\boldsymbol{y}_{0},(1-\\alpha)\\boldsymbol{I})$ concentrate around another ultra-thin shell around $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}(\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\boldsymbol{y}_{0},\\sqrt{(1-\\alpha)d})$, which has only a negligibly small intersection with $\\mathcal{S}$ and lies mostly in $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$. So, the initialization in cite86†Eq. 2.4 lies in $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$ with very high probability.\nL153: Given that the behavior of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ on $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$ can be wild, this initialization strategy is problematic.\nL154: ##### Our time-dependent warm-up strategy\nL155: \nL156: A typical flow of FM models takes the form\nL157: \nL158:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{x}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I}),$  |  | (3.1)\nL159: \nL160: where $\\alpha_{t}$ and $\\beta_{t}$ are known functions of $t$ with the property that\nL161:  | $\\displaystyle\\alpha_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\searrow}0,\\beta_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\nearrow}1\\;\\text{as}\\;t\\to 0,\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\nearrow}1,\\beta_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\searrow}0\\;\\text{as}\\;t\\to 1,$  |  | (3.2)\nL162: where $\\scriptstyle\\nearrow$ and $\\scriptstyle\\searrow$ indicate monotonically increasing and decreasing, respectively. Now, when $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ are close, $\\boldsymbol{x}=\\boldsymbol{y}+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ for some small (i.e., $\\norm{\\mb\\eps}$ is small compared to $\\norm{\\mb x}$ and $\\norm{\\mb z}$) but unknown $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. So, we can write the flow as\nL163:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\\alpha_{t}(\\boldsymbol{y}+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}=\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{y}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}+\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I}).$  |  | (3.3)\nL164: \nL165: To eliminate the unknown $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$, we can approximate the exact flow in cite88†Eq. 3.3 with the following approximate flow\nL166:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}\\approx\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{y}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$  |  | (3.4)\nL167: with an approximation error $\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. To control the error, (1) if $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is relatively large, a small $\\alpha_{t}$ is desirable; (2) if $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is already relatively small, a relatively large $\\alpha_{t}$ is acceptable. So, although we do not know $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ itself and hence its magnitude, with appropriate $\\alpha_{t}$ we can always make $\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ sufficiently small.\nL168: So, we leave $\\alpha_{t}$ as a learnable parameter. Since $\\alpha_{t}$ is a known function of $t$, we simply need to leave $t\\in[0,1]$ learnable, leading to our warm-start formulation\nL169:  | $\\displaystyle\\min\\nolimits_{\\boldsymbol{z},t\\in[0,1]}\\;\\ell\\pqty{\\mb y, \\mc A \\circ\\mc G_{\\mb\\theta} \\paren{\\alpha_t \\mb y + \\beta_t \\mb z, t}}.$  |  | (3.5)\nL170: Here we overload the notation of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ as $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\times[0,1]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{d}$—the second input is the current $t$ on the path (the notation in cite80†Eq. 2.3 assumes $t=0$).\nL171: In other words, due to the closeness of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$, we do not need to start from scratch, i.e., from a random sample drawn from the source distribution; instead, we plug $\\boldsymbol{y}$ into an appropriate, learnable time point of the flow to create a shortcut.\nL172: Our formulation in cite89†Eq. 3.5 can easily be generalized to latent FM models that are commonly used in practice—we just need to replace $\\mathcal{A}\\circ\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ with $\\mathcal{A}\\circ\\mathcal{D}\\circ\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ for the decoder $\\mathcal{D}$ in use. Moreover, it is not only grounded in theory and effective in practice (see cite22†Section 4 ), but also speeds up learning as $t>0$ implies shorter flows, although improving speed is not our current focus.\nL173: ##### Additional mean-variance calibration\nL174: Due to approximation errors in matching the ideal flow during FM training, as well as when approximating cite88†Eq. 3.3 using cite90†Eq. 3.4 , the distribution of $\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}$ could be slightly off the ideal distribution.\nL175: To rectify this, we perform a scalar mean-variance calibration in our implementation: we first draw $4000$ unconditional samples from the foundation FM model and estimate the scalar mean and variance of all coordinates for each time step on the FM model’s time grid; we then fit the data using a lightweight neural network, which predicts mean and variance as a continuous function of $t\\in[0,1]$, to be compatible with our continuous optimization in cite89†Eq. 3.5 . Our mean-variance calibration follows\nL176:  | $$\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{z}}_{t}=\\sqrt{\\sigma^{2}(Z_{t})/\\sigma^{2}(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})}\\cdot(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}-\\mu(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}))+\\mu(Z_{t}),$$  |  | (3.6)\nL177: \nL178: where $\\mu(Z_{t})$ and $\\sigma^{2}(Z_{t})$ are the scalar mean and variance predicted by the neural network, and $\\mu(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})$ and $\\sigma^{2}(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})$ are the scalar mean and variance for $\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}$ across all coordinates.\nL179: ### 3.2 A sharp Gaussianity regularization\nL180: \nL181: cite91†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Plot of the function $h(\\boldsymbol{z}_{0})$ (after a change of variable $u=\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}^{2}$). An ideal regularization function should blow up sharply away from the narrow concentration region in orange to promote Gaussianity effectively.\nL182: ##### Why is the Gaussian regularization in D-Flow problematic?\nL183: If $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$, $\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}^{2}\\sim\\chi^{2}(d)$ and the negative log-likelihood is $h(\\boldsymbol{z}_{0})=-(d/2-1)\\log\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}^{2}+\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}^{2}/2+C$ for some constant $C$ independent of $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}$. cite66†Ben-Hamu et al. (2024) promotes the Gaussianity of $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}$ by regularizing $h(\\boldsymbol{z}_{0})$.\nL184: While $h(\\boldsymbol{z}_{0})$ is minimized at any $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}$ satisfies $\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}=\\sqrt{d-2}$, away from this value the function changes painfully slowly; see cite92†Fig. 3 . For example, the function value only changes $\\leq 0.031\\%$ relative to the minimum in the $[62000,70000]$ range, much larger than the orange-highlighted CoM region.\nL185: This is problematic, as $\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}$ should concentrate sharply around $d$ and thus only functions that blow up quickly away from the $\\norm{\\mb z_0}_{2}=\\sqrt{d}$ level can effectively promote the Gaussianity of $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}$.\nL186: ##### Our sharp Gaussian regularization via an explicit constraint\nL187: \nL188: For cite89†Eq. 3.5 , we hope to promote the Gaussianity of $\\boldsymbol{z}$. To enforce the sharp concentration of $\\boldsymbol{z}$, we introduce the shell constraint\nL189: \nL190:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}_{\\varepsilon}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})\\doteq\\{\\boldsymbol{z}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:(1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\leq\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}\\leq(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\},\\quad\\text{with an}\\,\\varepsilon\\ll 1$  |  | (3.7)\nL191: as implied by cite87†Theorem 3.1 . So, our final formulation for the simple-distortion setting is\nL192: \nL193:  | $\\displaystyle\\boxed{\\min\\nolimits_{\\boldsymbol{z},t\\in[0,1]}\\;\\ell\\pqty{\\mb y, \\mc A \\circ\\mc G_{\\mb\\theta} \\paren{\\alpha_t \\mb y + \\beta_t \\mb z, t}}\\quad\\mathrm{s.t.}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}_{\\varepsilon}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})}.$  |  | (3.8)\nL194: \nL195: To ensure feasibility, in each iteration step to optimize the objective of cite93†Eq. 3.8 , we simply need to add the closed-form projection\nL196:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}_{\\varepsilon}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})}(\\boldsymbol{z})=\\begin{cases}(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{z}/\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}&\\text{if}\\ \\norm{\\mb z}_{2}\\geq(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\\\\nL197: (1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{z}/\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}&\\text{if}\\ \\norm{\\mb z}_{2}\\leq(1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}\\\\\nL198: \\boldsymbol{z}&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases},$  |  | (3.9)\nL199: where $\\mathcal{P}_{S}(\\cdot)$ denotes the Euclidean projection operator onto a set $S$. Using a spherical constraint $\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}=\\sqrt{d}$ or regularization to promote Gaussianity is not new in the FM and diffusion literature; see, e.g., cite94†Yang et al. (2024) . However, enforcing $\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}=\\sqrt{d}$ is a bit rigid as the actual length lies in a small range. Our shell constraint leaves reasonable slackness while still sharply encoding the Gaussianity.\nL200: We typically set $\\varepsilon=0.025$ in our implementation.\nL201: ### 3.3 Extension into the few-shot setting\nL202: We assume a small set of instances $\\left\\{\\boldsymbol{x}_{k}\\right\\}_{k=1,\\dots,K}$, some of which are close to the true $\\boldsymbol{x}$—realistic for many scientific domains with limited visual variability.\nL203: To adapt the time-dependent warm-start strategy in cite14†Section 3.1 to this setting, we consider linear combinations of $\\boldsymbol{x}_{k}$’s to take the place of $\\boldsymbol{y}$ for warm-start, i.e., starting with $\\alpha_{t}(\\sum\\nolimits_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}\\boldsymbol{x}_{k})+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}$, resulting in\nL204:  | $\\displaystyle\\boxed{\\min\\nolimits_{\\boldsymbol{z}\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}_{\\varepsilon}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d}),t\\in[0,1],\\boldsymbol{w}}\\;\\ell(\\boldsymbol{y},\\mathcal{A}\\circ\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}(\\alpha_{t}({\\textstyle\\sum\\nolimits_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}\\boldsymbol{x}_{k}})+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z},t))\\quad\\mathrm{s.t.}\\;\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\Delta^{K}}$  |  | (3.10)\nL205: to replace cite89†Eq. 3.5 .\nL206: Here, the simplex constraint $\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\Delta^{K}\\doteq\\left\\{\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}:\\boldsymbol{w}\\geq\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\intercal}\\boldsymbol{1}=1\\right\\}$ serves two purposes: (1) It fixes the scale of $\\boldsymbol{w}$, as the multiplicative relationship of $\\alpha_{t}$ and $\\boldsymbol{w}$ causes a scale ambiguity—e.g., we can scale $\\boldsymbol{w}$ by a factor $\\xi$ and scale $\\alpha_{t}$ by $1/\\xi$ to obtain the same $\\alpha_{t}(\\sum\\nolimits_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}\\boldsymbol{x}_{k})$, and vise versa; (2) It tends to promote sparsity in $\\boldsymbol{w}$ as an $\\ell_{1}$ constraint and hence select those instances close to the true $\\boldsymbol{x}$ automatically.\nL207: In actual implementation, we eliminate this constraint by a simple reparameterization $\\boldsymbol{w}=\\mathrm{softmax}(\\boldsymbol{v})$ and treat $\\boldsymbol{v}$ as an optimization variable. Since the proposed modification in warm-start does not affect $\\boldsymbol{z}$, our sharp Gaussian regularization in cite18†Section 3.2 can be directly integrated.\nL208: ## 4 Experiment\nL209: \nL210: Table 3: Results on simple-distortion IPs. (Bold: best, under: second best, CLIP: CLIPIQA)\nL211:  |  | AFHQ ($512\\times 512$)  | DIV2K ($512\\times 512$)  | RealSR ($512\\times 512$)\nL212: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL213: task  | method  | PSNR $\\uparrow$  | SSIM $\\uparrow$  | LPIPS $\\downarrow$  | CLIP $\\uparrow$  | PSNR $\\uparrow$  | SSIM $\\uparrow$  | LPIPS $\\downarrow$  | CLIP $\\uparrow$  | PSNR $\\uparrow$  | SSIM $\\uparrow$  | LPIPS $\\downarrow$  | CLIP $\\uparrow$\nL214: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL215: Super Resolution $\\times 4$  | DIP  | 29.85  | 0.78  | 0.37  | 0.33  | 25.75  | 0.73  | 0.42  | 0.40  | 26.81  | 0.72  | 0.44  | 0.30\nL216: FlowChef-P  | 29.23  | 0.79  | 0.38  | 0.64  | 25.08  | 0.71  | 0.43  | 0.60  | 25.89  | 0.71  | 0.43  | 0.44\nL217: FlowChef  | 29.25  | 0.79  | 0.38  | 0.65  | 25.09  | 0.71  | 0.43  | 0.60  | 25.92  | 0.71  | 0.43  | 0.44\nL218: FlowDPS-P  | 28.75  | 0.76  | 0.37  | 0.37  | 24.92  | 0.69  | 0.42  | 0.51  | 26.11  | 0.71  | 0.43  | 0.34\nL219: FlowDPS  | 28.60  | 0.75  | 0.42  | 0.35  | 24.83  | 0.68  | 0.45  | 0.46  | 26.10  | 0.70  | 0.45  | 0.32\nL220: D-Flow  | 26.37  | 0.70  | 0.54  | 0.31  | 23.42  | 0.64  | 0.52  | 0.37  | 23.60  | 0.62  | 0.53  | 0.28\nL221: FMPlug-W  | 30.13  | 0.81  | 0.34  | 0.18  | 25.77  | 0.74  | 0.38  | 0.24  | 26.58  | 0.73  | 0.39  | 0.16\nL222: FMPlug  | 30.31  | 0.81  | 0.33  | 0.20  | 25.88  | 0.74  | 0.38  | 0.27  | 26.66  | 0.74  | 0.38  | 0.17\nL223: Random Inpainting $70\\%$  | DIP  | 33.32  | 0.90  | 0.21  | 0.47  | 28.49  | 0.86  | 0.27  | 0.59  | 30.88  | 0.89  | 0.25  | 0.47\nL224: FlowChef-P  | 29.27  | 0.77  | 0.41  | 0.57  | 24.67  | 0.67  | 0.46  | 0.50  | 25.81  | 0.69  | 0.45  | 0.35\nL225: FlowChef  | 29.35  | 0.77  | 0.41  | 0.58  | 24.76  | 0.67  | 0.46  | 0.50  | 25.89  | 0.69  | 0.45  | 0.35\nL226: FlowDPS-P  | 27.63  | 0.73  | 0.41  | 0.43  | 24.01  | 0.65  | 0.47  | 0.54  | 25.68  | 0.69  | 0.47  | 0.36\nL227: FlowDPS  | 27.53  | 0.72  | 0.47  | 0.35  | 24.04  | 0.64  | 0.50  | 0.47  | 25.78  | 0.69  | 0.48  | 0.32\nL228: D-Flow  | 28.43  | 0.76  | 0.41  | 0.65  | 24.71  | 0.73  | 0.41  | 0.67  | 25.27  | 0.69  | 0.42  | 0.59\nL229: FMPlug-W  | 32.75  | 0.88  | 0.37  | 0.63  | 28.82  | 0.85  | 0.33  | 0.68  | 31.30  | 0.88  | 0.28  | 0.56\nL230: FMPlug  | 32.81  | 0.87  | 0.34  | 0.66  | 28.95  | 0.84  | 0.32  | 0.69  | 31.79  | 0.89  | 0.26  | 0.56\nL231: Gaussian Deblur  | DIP  | 29.39  | 0.77  | 0.39  | 0.30  | 25.23  | 0.70  | 0.43  | 0.38  | 26.17  | 0.70  | 0.46  | 0.28\nL232: FlowChef-P  | 23.84  | 0.63  | 0.54  | 0.28  | 20.41  | 0.49  | 0.62  | 0.23  | 21.42  | 0.51  | 0.63  | 0.19\nL233: FlowChef  | 23.87  | 0.63  | 0.54  | 0.28  | 20.41  | 0.49  | 0.62  | 0.23  | 21.42  | 0.51  | 0.63  | 0.19\nL234: FlowDPS-P  | 24.15  | 0.60  | 0.49  | 0.23  | 20.23  | 0.46  | 0.58  | 0.32  | 21.21  | 0.47  | 0.59  | 0.22\nL235: FlowDPS  | 23.69  | 0.58  | 0.55  | 0.15  | 20.22  | 0.45  | 0.61  | 0.20  | 21.21  | 0.47  | 0.61  | 0.17\nL236: D-Flow  | 25.90  | 0.66  | 0.54  | 0.34  | 23.64  | 0.64  | 0.52  | 0.37  | 23.65  | 0.60  | 0.54  | 0.30\nL237: FMPlug-W  | 30.38  | 0.79  | 0.40  | 0.22  | 26.05  | 0.72  | 0.43  | 0.29  | 27.05  | 0.72  | 0.44  | 0.21\nL238: FMPlug  | 30.41  | 0.79  | 0.39  | 0.21  | 26.26  | 0.73  | 0.41  | 0.28  | 27.22  | 0.73  | 0.43  | 0.19\nL239: Motion Deblur  | DIP  | 28.69  | 0.75  | 0.38  | 0.26  | 24.75  | 0.68  | 0.45  | 0.35  | 26.17  | 0.70  | 0.46  | 0.28\nL240: FlowChef-P  | 24.77  | 0.66  | 0.50  | 0.37  | 21.27  | 0.54  | 0.57  | 0.34  | 22.50  | 0.56  | 0.56  | 0.26\nL241: FlowChef  | 24.78  | 0.66  | 0.50  | 0.37  | 21.28  | 0.54  | 0.57  | 0.34  | 22.51  | 0.56  | 0.56  | 0.26\nL242: FlowDPS-P  | 24.81  | 0.64  | 0.46  | 0.28  | 21.07  | 0.51  | 0.54  | 0.39  | 22.50  | 0.55  | 0.55  | 0.27\nL243: FlowDPS  | 24.49  | 0.62  | 0.52  | 0.20  | 21.05  | 0.50  | 0.58  | 0.26  | 22.55  | 0.54  | 0.56  | 0.22\nL244: D-Flow  | 27.81  | 0.73  | 0.48  | 0.35  | 25.21  | 0.70  | 0.47  | 0.42  | 25.86  | 0.69  | 0.47  | 0.31\nL245: FMPlug-W  | 30.10  | 0.79  | 0.39  | 0.26  | 26.83  | 0.74  | 0.40  | 0.36  | 28.01  | 0.76  | 0.40  | 0.28\nL246: FMPlug  | 30.43  | 0.81  | 0.37  | 0.28  | 27.38  | 0.78  | 0.36  | 0.42  | 28.63  | 0.79  | 0.37  | 0.30\nL247: For brevity, we term our method FMPlug and benchmark its performance on both simple-distortion and few-shot IPs, in cite23†Section 4.1 and cite26†Section 4.2 , respectively. In cite27†Section 4.3 , we perform an ablation study to dissect the contributions of the two algorithmic components.\nL248: ### 4.1 Simple-distortion IPs\nL249: ##### Datasets, tasks, and evaluation metrics\nL250: We use 3 diverse datasets: DIV2K (cite95†Agustsson and Timofte, 2017 ), RealSR (cite96†Cai et al., 2019 ) and AFHQ (cite97†Choi et al., 2020 ), and take $100$ random images out of each dataset. We set the image resolution to $512\\times 512$ by resizing and cropping the original.\nL251: We consider four linear IPs: i) 4$\\times$ super-resolution from $128\\times 128$ to $512\\times 512$; ii) $70\\%$ random-mask inpainting; iii) Gaussian deblurring with a kernel size of $61$ and standard deviation of $3.0$; iv) Motion deblurring with a kernel size of $61$ and intensity of $0.5$. We add Gaussian noise $\\sigma=0.03$ to all measurements.\nL252: For evaluation metrics, we use PSNR for pixel-level difference, SSIM and DISTS for structural and textural similarity, LPIPS for perceptual difference, and CLIPIQA & MUSIQ as no-reference quality metrics.\nL253: ##### Competing methods\nL254: We compare our FMPlug (-W: warm-start only, Number of Function Evaluations (NFE) $=3$) with deep image prior (DIP) (cite98†Ulyanov et al., 2020 ) (an untrained image prior) + cite80†Eq. 2.3 , D-Flow ($\\text{NFE}=6$) (cite66†Ben-Hamu et al., 2024 ) (a SOTA plug-in method), FlowDPS ($\\text{NFE}=28$) (cite64†Kim et al., 2025 ) (a SOTA interleaving method) and FlowChef ($\\text{NFE}=28$) (cite65†Patel et al., 2024 ) (another SOTA interleaving method).\nL255: For a fair comparison, we use Stable Diffusion V3 (cite62†Patrick Esser et al, 2024 ) as the backbone for all methods that require foundation priors. We also compare with the recent OT-ODE  (cite73†Pokle et al., 2023 ), PnP-Flow  (cite67†Martin et al., 2025 ) based on a domain-specific FM prior, AFHQ-Cat from (cite67†Martin et al., 2025 )—not with foundation priors, however, because OT-ODE and PnP-Flow are not compatible with latent FM models.\nL256: For methods that integrate text prompts, including FlowDPS and FlowChef, we compare two variants with the prompts on and off, respectively; we use postfix -P to indicate the prompt-enabled variants. We use the pretrained degradation-aware prompt extractor of cite99†Wu et al. (2024) to generate label-style text prompts. We set the CFG scale to $2.0$ when text prompts are on. Details of the hyperparameter can be found in cite30†Section A.1 .\nL257: cite100†Table 3 summarizes a part of the quantitative results; details and visualizations can be found in cite35†Section A.4 . We can observe that: (1) Our FMPlug is the overall winner by all metrics but CLIPIQA and MUSIQ, the no-reference metrics, beating the untrained DIP—a strong baseline.\nL258: FlowChef and FlowDPS, with and without text prompts, lag behind even the untrained DIP by large margins and generate visually blurry and oversmooth images as shown in cite101†Fig. 4 , highlighting the general struggle of interleaving methods to ensure simultaneous measurement and manifold feasibility; (2) For plug-in methods, our FMPlug improves upon D-Flow—our main competitor, by considerable margins based on all metrics but CLIPIQA, showing the solid advantage of our warm-start strategy and Gaussian regularization over theirs; and (3) FMPlug further improves PSNR and SSIM slightly over FMPlug-W, with the largest improvement seen in CLIPIQA, showing stronger visual quality.\nL259: This confirms the benefits brought about by the sharp Gaussianity regularization.\nL260: cite102†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Visual comparison of results in Gaussian deblurring. Table 4: Gaussian Deblur and Super Resolution $4\\times$ on AFHQ-Cat $256\\times 256$ with additive Gaussian noise ($\\sigma=0.03$). FD: Foundation; DS: Domain-specific; Bold: best, under: second best; -: not available\nL261:  | Super Resolution $4\\times$  | Gaussian Blur\nL262: --- | --- | ---\nL263:  | LPIPS$\\downarrow$  | PSNR$\\uparrow$  | SSIM$\\uparrow$  | DIST$\\downarrow$  | CLIPIQA$\\uparrow$  | MUSIQ$\\uparrow$  | LPIPS$\\downarrow$  | PSNR$\\uparrow$  | SSIM$\\uparrow$  | DIST$\\downarrow$  | CLIPIQA$\\uparrow$  | MUSIQ$\\uparrow$\nL264: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n"
